    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>'s Islamic reform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of 1804, the Sokoto Caliphate became the largest state in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</li><li>Some thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> shared one Islamic legal culture, a decentralised design that survived for almost a century without succession crises.</li><li>British forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Maxim guns</a> conquered it in 1903, then governed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The last independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, Attahiru I, answered the British conquest with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">hijra</a> eastwards that deliberately echoed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">dan Fodio</a>'s founding migration.</p></aside>
    </section>

